.TH FKSTRCMP 3 2026-09-24 "fkstring" "fkstring Library Manual"
.nh
.SH NAME
fkstrcmp, fkstrcasecmp, fkstreq \- compare fkstrings
.SH LIBRARY
fkstring library
.RI ( libfkstring ,\~ \-lfkstring )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fkstring.h>
.PP
.BI "int fkstrcmp(const fkstring *" a ", const fkstring *" b );
.BI "int fkstrcasecmp(const fkstring *" a ", const fkstring *" b );
.BI "int fkstreq(const fkstring *" a ", const fkstring *" b );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fkstrcmp ()
compares
.I a
and
.I b
byte by byte, as
.B unsigned char
values like
.BR memcmp (3),
over the length of the shorter string.
If those bytes are equal, the shorter string sorts first.
Unlike
.BR strcmp (3)
on the
.I cstr
fields, it compares past embedded NUL bytes, and it is safe on empty
strings.
.PP
.BR fkstrcasecmp ()
is like
.BR fkstrcmp (),
but treats the ASCII letters
.BR A \(en Z
as
.BR a \(en z .
Unlike
.BR strcasecmp (3),
it does not depend on the locale, and bytes outside the ASCII range are
never folded.
.PP
A NULL argument sorts before any
.BR fkstring ,
including an empty one; two NULL arguments compare equal.
.PP
.BR fkstreq ()
tests
.I a
and
.I b
for equality.
When their lengths differ it returns at once, without reading their
contents.
Two NULL arguments are equal; NULL never equals an
.BR fkstring .
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR fkstrcmp ()
and
.BR fkstrcasecmp ()
return \-1, 0, or 1 as
.I a
sorts before, the same as, or after
.IR b .
Unlike
.BR strcmp (3),
the result is always one of these three values.
.PP
.BR fkstreq ()
returns 1 if
.I a
and
.I b
have the same length and contents, and 0 otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
fkstring *a = fkstrnew("Hello"), *b = fkstrnew("hello");
\&
fkstreq(a, b);        /* 0 */
fkstrcmp(a, b);       /* \-1: \(aqH\(aq (0x48) < \(aqh\(aq (0x68) */
fkstrcasecmp(a, b);   /* 0 */
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fkstring (3),
.BR fkstartswith (3),
.BR memcmp (3)
